R3. Fields, in order, are minute (0..=59), hour (0..=23), day of month (1..=31), month (1..=12), day of week (0..=6, Sunday=0). Sunday 7 is invalid; there is no normalization.

R4. First split input on the literal ASCII space byte, retaining empty fields. If the number of pieces is not five, return FieldCount with that number before inspecting any field. Empty input therefore has found=1; tabs and newlines are not separators.

R5. Validate fields in the given order and stop at the first failing field. Every offset in ParseError is a zero-based UTF-8 byte offset within that field, never within the whole expression.

R6. Before parsing any item in a field, scan its entire bytes left to right. Only ASCII digits, ASCII letters A..Z and a..z, *, -, /, and comma are legal. Return InvalidCharacter at the first other byte, even if an earlier item in the same field has a different error.

R7. Split a field on commas retaining empty items. Process items left to right. For an empty item (including an empty whole field) return EmptyItem at its starting offset; a trailing empty item starts at the field byte length.

R8. Each nonempty item has one base, optionally followed by one slash and one step. Base is exactly *, an atom, or atom-atom. An atom is a decimal number or a permitted field-specific name from R29. Reject every other structure as InvalidSyntax at the item start BEFORE reading any numeric values. This includes empty slash operands, multiple slashes, multiple hyphens, and starred range endpoints.

R9. Numbers are nonempty ASCII decimal sequences. A multi-digit number beginning with 0 yields LeadingZero at that number start, before overflow or bounds checks. Single 0 is permitted wherever bounds permit it.

R10. Accumulate each number as u32 using checked arithmetic. Overflow yields NumberOverflow at the number start. For numeric bases and endpoints, values fitting u32 but outside the field domain yield OutOfRange at that number start; step bounds instead follow R12.

R11. For numeric bases, validate start then end (if present), including each number's leading-zero, overflow and domain checks. Then if start>end return ReversedRange at the end number start. These errors precede all step-number errors.

R12. For step numbers, apply leading-zero and u32 overflow checks, then require 1..=the full field domain width (60,24,31,12,7). Otherwise return BadStep at the step number start, including step 0. Steps larger than the selected range length but within the domain width are valid.

R13. An unstepped * selects the full domain. An unstepped number selects just itself. An unstepped range selects both endpoints and all intervening values.

R14. A stepped * starts at the domain minimum and ends at its maximum. A stepped range starts at its lower endpoint and ends at its upper endpoint. A stepped single number extends from that number through the domain maximum, unlike an unstepped single number.

R15. Step selection includes the start, then start+step repeatedly while within the end. Values are the union of all list items; repetitions and overlaps are harmless.

R16. allowed(field) returns selected values exactly once, in ascending numeric order. It must not depend on list-item order.

R17. A syntactically valid schedule need not be realizable as a date; parsing accepts, for example, February with only day 30 selected.

R18. Cron equality includes both the selected sets and whether each day field was exactly the one-byte string *. Thus * and */1 in a day field compare unequal; other syntax differences selecting identical sets compare equal.

R19. Additional exact API: #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)] pub struct CivilDateTime { pub year: u16, pub month: u8, pub day: u8, pub hour: u8, pub minute: u8 }. #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)] pub enum DatePart { Year, Month, Day, Hour, Minute }. #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)] pub enum DateError { InvalidDateTime { component: DatePart } }. impl Cron { pub fn matches(&self, at: CivilDateTime) -> Result<bool, DateError>; pub fn next_fire(&self, after: CivilDateTime) -> Result<Option<CivilDateTime>, DateError>; }.

R20. CivilDateTime is a minute-resolution proleptic Gregorian civil value. Supported years are 2000..=2399, months 1..=12, days 1..=that actual month length, hours 0..=23, minutes 0..=59. There are no clocks, offsets, seconds, or timezones.

R21. Validate any datetime argument before using schedule membership: first Year, then Month, then Day, then Hour, then Minute. Return DateError::InvalidDateTime for the first invalid component, even for schedules that cannot fire.

R22. Gregorian leap years are divisible by 4 except century years not divisible by 400. February has 29 days in leap years and 28 otherwise; April, June, September, November have 30 and other months have 31.

R23. Weekdays use Sunday=0 through Saturday=6; 2000-01-01 is Saturday (6). Advance weekday by one for each actual civil day, including leap days.

R24. matches returns true exactly when minute, hour and month are selected and the date satisfies the day-field rules in R32 and R33.

R25. next_fire returns the earliest matching supported datetime STRICTLY later than after. Exclude after even if it matches; allow another minute on its date. Return None when no later matching datetime exists through 2399-12-31 23:59.

R26. Search crosses actual month/year boundaries and skips nonexistent dates; never clamp an invalid day-of-month to a shorter month. An unrealizable schedule returns None, and at the maximum supported datetime next_fire always returns None.

R27. All calendar and search methods are read-only: repeated calls on the same Cron and datetime have identical results, and parsing or querying another Cron cannot change an existing instance.

R28. ASCII letters are lexically legal in every field even where no names are accepted: lexically valid but disallowed names produce InvalidSyntax under R30. Non-ASCII letters still yield InvalidCharacter according to the whole-field lexical precedence in R6.

R29. In Month bases and range endpoints accept the exact three-letter ASCII names JAN,FEB,MAR,APR,MAY,JUN,JUL,AUG,SEP,OCT,NOV,DEC for 1..12; in DayOfWeek accept SUN,MON,TUE,WED,THU,FRI,SAT for 0..6. Names are ASCII case-insensitive. Named and numeric range endpoints may be mixed.

R30. Any base/endpoint that is neither all digits nor an exact allowed name makes the entire item InvalidSyntax at item start, before all numeric validations. Names are forbidden in Minute, Hour, DayOfMonth and in steps. Full names, prefixes, or mixtures of letters and digits are invalid syntax.

R31. The syntax check in R8 now treats a permitted name like a number. Numeric bounds, range ordering and step handling are otherwise unchanged; named endpoints have their mapped numeric values. Step values remain decimal numbers only.

R32. A day field is unrestricted ONLY if its original field text is exactly *. If both day fields are unrestricted the date matches; if only one is unrestricted, require membership in the other. */1 and lists covering the full domain are restricted.

R33. If both day fields are restricted, require day-of-month membership OR weekday membership, except on the LAST actual civil day of each month, when BOTH memberships are required. This exception uses actual month length, including leap years; it applies even if a restricted field happens to select its whole domain.

R34. Minute, hour and month membership remain mandatory regardless of the day-field rule. Apply the same day rule in matches and next_fire; the last-day exception never changes time selection or calendar validation.

R35. Additional exact API: #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)] pub enum WindowError { InvalidStart { component: DatePart }, InvalidEnd { component: DatePart }, ReversedWindow, ZeroCap }. #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)] pub struct FireWindow { pub fires: Vec<CivilDateTime>, pub truncated: bool }. impl Cron { pub fn previous_fire(&self, before: CivilDateTime) -> Result<Option<CivilDateTime>, DateError>; pub fn fires_between(&self, start: CivilDateTime, end: CivilDateTime, cap: usize) -> Result<FireWindow, WindowError>; pub fn canonical(&self) -> String; }.

R36. If input begins with the ASCII byte @, dispatch special strings BEFORE field splitting or lexical checks. Accept only exactly @hourly, @daily, @weekly, @monthly, @yearly (lowercase, no whitespace). Every other such input returns UnknownSpecial, including a valid name with a trailing space. @ elsewhere follows normal field parsing.

R37. Special expansions are @hourly = 0 * * * *, @daily = 0 0 * * *, @weekly = 0 0 * * 0, @monthly = 0 0 1 * *, @yearly = 0 0 1 1 *. Parsing a special must equal parsing its expansion, including day-field unrestricted flags.

R38. canonical prints exactly five fields separated by one ASCII space, with no leading/trailing whitespace. No names, macros, ranges or arbitrary steps are printed. Each partial domain is printed as its selected decimal numbers in ascending order separated by commas, with no leading zeros or duplicates.

R39. For a full domain in Minute, Hour or Month, canonical prints *. For a day field originally unrestricted, print *. For a full-domain RESTRICTED day field, print */1, the sole exception to the no-steps canonical rule. This preserves original unrestricted flags.

R40. parse_cron(canonical()) must equal the original Cron and preserve matches, search and canonical output; canonical(canonical-parsed) is identical. Canonical formatting depends on selected sets and day flags, never on original spelling or special-string use.

R41. previous_fire is the mirror of next_fire: validate before using membership, return the latest matching supported datetime STRICTLY earlier than before, or None if none exists at or after 2000-01-01 00:00. It uses exactly the same day rules and calendar bounds.

R42. fires_between validates start first (component precedence from R21) and maps failures to InvalidStart; then validates end and maps failures to InvalidEnd. Both validations precede all window ordering and cap checks. Next, if start>end return ReversedWindow; next, if cap==0 return ZeroCap (even for an empty window).

R43. fires_between uses the half-open interval [start,end): include a matching start and exclude end. start==end with positive cap returns empty fires and truncated=false. Return the earliest up to cap matching datetimes in strictly increasing chronological order, without duplicates.

R44. Window truncated is true exactly when there is at least one additional matching datetime in [start,end) beyond the returned cap entries. Filling the cap exactly with no further match must leave truncated=false. Empty windows and windows with no match have truncated=false.

R45. Window calls and previous_fire are read-only under R27. Windows may span the entire supported year range and have arbitrary positive usize caps; stop collecting after cap and detecting the first extra match. Avoid minute-by-minute scans across the whole 400-year range: jump or iterate by civil dates with selected hours/minutes so impossible schedules complete promptly.

